X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Libcal Bccls 10306": "https://bccls.libcal.com/event/17392023"}
X-SOURCE-ID:libcal_bccls_10306
X-SOURCE:Libcal Bccls 10306
DTSTART:20261014T180000Z
DTEND:20261014T193000Z
DTSTAMP:20261014T000000Z
SUMMARY:The Winding History of School Sports: A Unique American Invention NEW
DESCRIPTION:This is a paid Adult School class taking place at the Yogi 
 Berra Museum. Click here to register.\n\nWhy do American schools build 
 stadiums\, award athletic scholarships\, pay coaches extravagant salaries 
 even as academic programs get cut\, and turn teenagers into campus heroes - 
 while most of the world keeps sports completely separate from education? 
 This lively talk explores the surprising history behind the uniquely 
 American idea of the "student-athlete". By comparing the US system with the 
 European model of independent sports clubs\, we'll uncover how this unusual 
 partnership began\, why it became so powerful\, and what it means for 
 education - and the futures - of young athletes today.
LOCATION:Montclair - Offsite\, Montclair Public Library
ORGANIZER;CN="Montclair Adult School":MAILTO:adultschool@montclairlibrary.org
CATEGORIES:Adult School, Lectures / History & Culture
UID:LibCal-10306-17392023
URL:https://bccls.libcal.com/event/17392023
END:VEVENT
